{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794586882,"nonce":"tzkiS0OBkC9QDyBO/xR9WqHXi4Q=","message":"Slice 1 landed locally in a4e6f240 (not pushed).\n\n`ash --build --target aarch64-apple-ios-sim` and `aarch64-apple-ios` now link with Xcode alone:\n- The compiler and SDK come from `xcrun`; no variable is set. A bare `CC` is ignored for these targets, since it names the host's compiler (the spike shell had `CC=/usr/bin/clang`, which first made the link fail); `ASH_LINKER_\u003ctriple\u003e` still overrides.\n- The object is written under `arm64-apple-ios14.0-simulator` (or the device spelling), so it records its platform. `IPHONEOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET` sets the minimum.\n- The program always links the static runtime and `-framework CoreFoundation`.\n- std/build.rs takes the target's headers from the same SDK, so building the runtime needs no `BINDGEN_EXTRA_CLANG_ARGS_*`.\n\nChecked: hello world and ten test programs, built only with `ash --build`, print what the interpreter prints on the iOS simulator, exit 0. The linked binary's Mach-O platform is IOSSIMULATOR. `cargo test -p ash --test mobile_link` covers the link and the platform tag (skips when Xcode or the runtime archive is absent).\n\nAcceptance items 1, 2 and 4 (the executable form) are met; item 3 for the device and the arm64 simulator at the object level (a device link was not run: no device runtime archive built yet; the x86_64 simulator is untried). Still open: item 4's static library / XCFramework form, item 5 (entry glue), item 6 (`ash run`, CI), per-target runtimes in the release workflow, and static native binding.","files":null}]}